%I A399174 #34 Nov 11 2026 10:11:43
%S A399174 5,7,11,13,17,8741,8747,8753,8779,9461,9463,9467,9473,9533,9539,9697,
%T A399174 9721,9733,92861,92863,92867,92893,93077,93083,93089,93139,93151,
%U A399174 93187,93199,93229,93241,93251,93253,93257,94397,94399,94439,94463,94477,94513,94529,94541
%N A399174 Primes p for which pi_{24;19,23}(p) - pi_{24;1,5}(p) = -1, where pi_{m;a,b,...}(x) is the number of primes <= x which are congruent to a or b or ... (mod m).
%C A399174 From _Leandro Hector Gallego_ and _Jianing Song_: (Start)
%C A399174 This sequence is about one of the 21 prime races modulo 24 between cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see A401958 for more details. Specifically, here we have H = {1,5} and S = {19,23}, corresponding to T = {chi_{-4}, chi_{-24}}, where chi_d = (d/.) is the Kronecker symbol viewed as a character modulo N = 24.
%C A399174 This sequence gives primes p such that Sum_{primes q<=p} (chi_{-4}(q) + chi_{-24}(q))/2 = 1.
%C A399174 Assuming GRH and LI (see A401958), the set of x with pi_{24;19,23}(x) > pi_{24;1,5}(x) has logarithmic density 0.98391, making the race between {1,5} and {19,23} the 17th most biased among the 21 races of cosets of index 4 in (Z/24Z)*; see the Zenodo preprint below.
%C A399174 Let D(x) = pi_{24;19,23}(x) - pi_{24;1,5}(x). Below x0 = 7*10^12 there are respectively 30659637567, 30659688364, 30659698140, 30659716342, 30659718072, 30659718286, 30659752652, and 30659759379 primes congruent to 1, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, or 23 modulo 24, giving D(x0) = 186100. There are 12 sign-changing zones giving a total of 200480 terms below x0:
%C A399174   (1) a(1..5) = 5-17;
%C A399174   (2) a(6..9) = 8741-8779 (there are 5 primes a(6) <= p <= a(9) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -2 at p = 8761);
%C A399174   (3) a(10..18) = 9461-9733 (there are 9 primes a(10) <= p <= a(18) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -1 at p = 9461);
%C A399174   (4) a(19..66) = 92861-97459 (there are 151 primes a(19) <= p <= a(66) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -6 at p = 96601);
%C A399174   (5) a(67..106) = 179261-185309 (there are 325 primes a(67) <= p <= a(106) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -7 at p = 181777);
%C A399174   (6) a(107..804) = 89340217-90631313 (there are 9813 primes a(107) <= p <= a(804) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -46 at p = 89589361);
%C A399174   (7) a(805..4964) = 1258218677-1273024121 (there are 400574 primes a(805) <= p <= a(4964) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -188 at p = 1260950261);
%C A399174   (8) a(4965..18700) = 18864277013-19452468583 (there are 2119955 primes a(4965) <= p <= a(18700) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -401 at p = 19430793241);
%C A399174   (9) a(18701..33423) = 41719021417-43850633227 (there are 18372164 primes a(18701) <= p <= a(33423) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -1825 at p = 43589268629);
%C A399174   (10) a(33424..76356) = 47818422077-49836494777 (there are 44167148 primes a(33424) <= p <= a(76356) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -2724 at p = 48745060349);
%C A399174   (11) a(76357..153045) = 850966408489-875835918031 (there are 439197574 primes a(76357) <= p <= a(153045) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -6235 at p = 853793023301);
%C A399174   (12) a(153046..200480) = 1018491958177-1083989871589 (there are 92979494 primes a(153046) <= p <= a(200480) such that D(p) < 0, with D(p) reaching a minimum of -5095 at p = 1083545336477).
%C A399174 The total number of primes p <= x0 with D(p) < 0 is 597247217. (End)
%H A399174 Jianing Song, <a href="/A399174/b399174.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..76356</a> (the first 10 sign-changing zones)
%H A399174 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.24020025">Logarithmic densities of prime number races between the cosets of a subgroup of index 4 modulo 24</a>, preprint, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A399174 Leandro Hector Gallego, <a href="/A399174/a399174_1.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..200480</a> (all terms below 7*10^12; a(1)-a(76356) from Jianing Song, a(76357)-a(153852) corrected by Leandro Hector Gallego).
%H A399174 Greg Martin and Daniel Fiorilli, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Chennai-31Aug10.pdf">Prime number races: An asymptotic formula for the densities</a>, Analytic and Combinatorial Number Theory, Institute of Mathematical Sciences, Chennai, India. August 31, 2010.
%H A399174 Greg Martin, <a href="https://personal.math.ubc.ca/~gerg/slides/Canberra-29Nov18.pdf">Prime number races</a>, Pure Math Seminar, UNSW Canberra. November 29, 2018.
%H A399174 Andrey S. Shchebetov, <a href="https://math101.guru/wp-content/uploads/2018/09/01-A3-Presentation-v7.3EN-no.pdf">Testing Chebyshev's bias for prime numbers up to 10^15</a>, EUCYS 2018.
%H A399174 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chebyshev%27s_bias">Chebyshev's bias</a>.
%o A399174 (PARI) my(k=0); forprime(p=2, 1e11, if(p%24==1||p%24==5, k--); if(p%24==19||p%24==23, k++); if(k==-1, print1(p, ", ")))
